<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tevaram</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as தேவாரம், Tēvāram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tevaram is the name given to the first seven volumes of the Tirumurai, the twelve-volume canon of Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>: 796 hymns of 8,284 stanzas by the three most prominent Shaiva poet-saints, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a> and Sundarar. The &quot;Tevaram trio&quot; are counted among the 63 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a>, the revered poet-saints of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The three poet-saints lived, by the dating of Peterson and Prentiss, between the 6th and 8th centuries CE, or by Champakalakshmi's in the 7th to 9th; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a> probably overlapped between 570 and 670, and Sundarar lived in the late 7th or early 8th century. In the Pallava period they travelled across the Tamil country, singing in temples and public places at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, Jains and Buddhists competed for royal and urban patronage. Their lives are known mainly from later hagiographies, chiefly the Periya Puranam, full of miracles and of Jain persecutors, which has made their reliability and even their centuries a matter of controversy.</p><p>The hymns long passed on mainly by word of mouth. Champakalakshmi sees three stages: composition by the saints, adoption in temple ritual by patikam singers, and deliberate arrangement into a canon, which monastic pontiffs folded into the Shaiva Siddhanta canon in the 13th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Volumes I to III hold the 383 or 384 hymns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a>, IV to VI the 313 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a>, and VII the 100 of Sundarar. Most hymns, or patikams, have ten or eleven verses, each a four-line stanza with a refrain, and the last verse often carries the poet's signature. They are set to 23 of the 103 pan modes of ancient Tamil music, to be sung with a stringed instrument such as the yal. The scholar Zvelebil quotes a Tamil saying: my Appar sang of me, Sambandar sang of himself, Sundarar sang of women. The hymns show that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nataraja">Nataraja</a> image and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> linga were already established, and they name the temples later counted as the 275 Paadal Petra Sthalams.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Tradition holds that Raja Raja Chola I (985–1013), hearing excerpts at court, sent the priest Nambiyandar Nambi to recover the hymns, which were found as palm leaves half eaten by white ants in a chamber of the Chidambaram Nataraja temple, and that the king overcame the opposition of the temple's Dikshitars, earning the name Tirumurai Kanda Cholan. The Madras Tamil Lexicon calls the collection a Tamil Veda. Inscriptions from the 8th century record hymn singers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temples; Rajaraja maintained 48 of them, called pidarars, and from the 13th century the odhuvars took over the singing, as they still do after the daily rituals in the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tevaram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Tevaram</b> of Cuntaramurti the poet asks Shiva, “Do you know proper Tamil?”; the scholar David Shulman reads the question as one about knowing how to love, and notes that to “know Tamil” could also mean to be a civilised being.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the Nayanars, Karaikkal Ammaiyar, probably of the 6th century, was one of the earliest; Appar and Sambandar in the 7th century and Sundarar after them composed the hymns later collected as the <b>Tevaram</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Tevaram</b> is the name given to the first seven volumes of the Tirumurai, the twelve-volume canon of Tamil Shaivism: 796 hymns of 8,284 stanzas by the three most prominent Shaiva poet-saints, Sambandar, Appar and Sundarar.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tevaram">Tevaram</a></li></ul></section>
